From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 16:24:22 +0000
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: wire the FRITZ!Box 7560 LEDs, buttons and DECT
 reset

Now that the SoC dtsi describes the Equilibrium pin controller and its
two GPIO banks, attach the board's GPIO consumers.

All nine lines come from AVM's own board description,
arch/mips/boot/dts/lantiq/avm/grx350_common.dtsi in the 4.9 BSP, which
exports each of them individually through the out-of-tree
"avm,avm_gpio_generic" shim under the &avm_gpio node. The shim is not
ported -- gpio-leds, gpio-keys-polled and gpio-hog cover everything it
provided -- but the bank, pin number and active level are transcribed
unchanged:

  gpio_avm_led_power       gpio1  4 HIGH  ->  green:power
  gpio_avm_led_internet    gpio0 18 HIGH  ->  green:internet
  gpio_avm_led_festnetz    gpio0  3 HIGH  ->  green:festnetz
  gpio_avm_led_wlan        gpio0 14 HIGH  ->  green:wlan
  gpio_avm_led_info        gpio0  0 HIGH  ->  green:info
  gpio_avm_led_info_red    gpio1  2 HIGH  ->  red:info
  gpio_avm_button_dect     gpio0  7 LOW   ->  KEY_PHONE
  gpio_avm_button_wlan     gpio0  2 LOW   ->  KEY_RFKILL
  gpio_avm_dect_reset      gpio0 10 HIGH  ->  hogged output-high

Bank-1 pins 4 and 2 are SoC pins 36 and 34, which the vendor tree also
lists in its "vcodec" pin group, and green:festnetz sits on pin 3, which
the vendor tree lists under "25Mhz". Those groups are unused on this
board and are not declared in our pinctrl node, so claiming the pads as
GPIOs muxes them to mux value 0, which is what the LEDs need.

The keys use gpio-keys-polled because the GPIO banks intentionally do
not act as an interrupt controller; a 100 ms poll interval is well
inside what both short and long presses need.

The power LED defaults to "on": the lamp is already lit when the kernel
starts, and blinking it off until userspace comes up would read as a
failed boot.
led-boot / led-running / led-failsafe / led-upgrade / led-wifi aliases
follow the OpenWrt convention so the generic diag.sh drives them
without a board-specific hook.

The DECT reset line is hogged asserted. There is no DECT driver in this
tree, so asserting the reset keeps the chip quiesced rather than
half-initialised.

USB VBUS (gpio0 6) and the PCIe reset lines (gpio0 8, 9, 16) are left
unclaimed on purpose; they belong to the USB and PCIe phases and are
noted as such in the source.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
index 6521ce1d66cb..5712bba4be3b 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@
 
 #include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/mips-gic.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/clock/intel,grx500-clk.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/input/input.h>
 
 #include "xrx500.dtsi"
 #include "seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi"
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi
index eed611b4e9b1..fc303f1393ed 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi
@@ -81,3 +81,98 @@ reserved-memory {
 		ranges;
 	};
 };
+
+/ {
+	leds {
+		compatible = "gpio-leds";
+
+		/*
+		 * Bank 1, pin 4 == SoC pin 36. Lit as soon as the driver
+		 * probes. The lamp is already on when the kernel starts, and
+		 * blinking it off mid-boot would read as a failed boot.
+		 * The led-boot / led-running aliases hand it to userspace
+		 * afterwards.
+		 */
+		led_power: led-0 {
+			label = "green:power";
+			gpios = <&gpio1 4 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+			default-state = "on";
+		};
+
+		led_internet: led-1 {
+			label = "green:internet";
+			gpios = <&gpio0 18 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+			default-state = "off";
+		};
+
+		led_festnetz: led-2 {
+			label = "green:festnetz";
+			gpios = <&gpio0 3 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+			default-state = "off";
+		};
+
+		led_wlan: led-3 {
+			label = "green:wlan";
+			gpios = <&gpio0 14 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+			default-state = "off";
+		};
+
+		led_info: led-4 {
+			label = "green:info";
+			gpios = <&gpio0 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+			default-state = "off";
+		};
+
+		/* Bank 1, pin 2 == SoC pin 34. */
+		led_info_red: led-5 {
+			label = "red:info";
+			gpios = <&gpio1 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+			default-state = "off";
+		};
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Polled rather than interrupt-driven: the GPIO banks do not
+	 * publish an irq domain (see xrx500.dtsi). 100 ms is fast enough
+	 * for both the short-press and the long-press handlers.
+	 */
+	keys {
+		compatible = "gpio-keys-polled";
+		poll-interval = <100>;
+
+		button-dect {
+			label = "dect";
+			linux,code = <KEY_PHONE>;
+			gpios = <&gpio0 7 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+
+		button-wlan {
+			label = "wlan";
+			linux,code = <KEY_RFKILL>;
+			gpios = <&gpio0 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+	};
+
+	aliases {
+		led-boot = &led_power;
+		led-running = &led_power;
+		led-failsafe = &led_info_red;
+		led-upgrade = &led_info;
+		led-wifi = &led_wlan;
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * DECT reset, active high. No DECT driver exists in this tree, so the
+ * line is hogged and driven asserted at boot to hold the Dialog DECT
+ * companion chip quiesced instead of leaving it half-initialised and
+ * chattering on the shared SSI bus.
+ */
+&gpio0 {
+	dect-reset-hog {
+		gpio-hog;
+		gpios = <10 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		output-high;
+		line-name = "dect-reset";
+	};
+};
